[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked **known: one issue, one fix queued**. Its own data says
otherwise. The reports merged into it come from three different problems, and the parser fix
closed only one of them.

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October:

- `parser`: **fixed**, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Ticket #1 closed.
- `hint`: **won't fix**, the `booking-v3` form is retired. Ticket #2 closed.
- `one-day`: **not addressed**. Ticket #3 stays open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day problem is not fixed. Send new one-day reports to #3, not to
#4471.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant in #4471 writes
  the day first, so any date with a day of 12 or less comes out swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July.
  This is the fix that is queued.
- **`hint`**: the `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what
  the hint says, and the form reads it day first. The date is swapped at entry, before the parser
  sees it, so the parser fix leaves these broken. Only `form` reports from tenants on
  `booking-v3` land here.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, which a day/month swap cannot do. The invite shows
  Monday instead of Tuesday, one client sees a different day from another in a different time
  zone, one invite arrived before its booking. That points at time-zone handling, not the
  parser. The desk already noted these reports; the table on #4471 only footnoted them.

## Why the #4471 page shows one cause

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table on #4471 counts the same reports, but it keeps one-day moves inside `parser` and marks
`hint` as "no ticket of its own". The intake tags do not help either: intake guesses from the
reporter's browser, so en-US browsers get tagged `hint` wherever the report came from. The cause
depends on the tenant and the way in (`#lib/kb/causes.ts`), not on the browser.

Merging by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) is not what
merged these: the subjects vary. They were merged because they all looked like "wrong date".

## Lessons

- A count is not a cause. Before you mark a big ticket known, sort it by cause and read the
  reports that do not fit.
- A footnote under a row is a problem without a ticket. Give it one.

